/-!
# The executable's entry

Lean owns the process. `Editor.Main.run` is what the executable's `main`
calls: it acts on the mode the command line selects
(`Editor/Executable.lean`, which pins the dispatch as a pure function),
handing the hosts the packages, the input scheme and the config base it is
given.

`editor demo` only shows the build works: it calls the Rust library and
prints what comes back, and it runs one command against the library through
the text port's real binding.

`editor --version` says the version, the build ID and the commit of the
running build, read from its bundle's `Info.plist`, or that it is a
development build when it runs from outside a bundle
(`Editor/Version.lean`).

`editor --probe` is what a restart runs before it hands the session over
(`Editor/Restore/Restart.lean`). By the time `run` is called every module
initialiser has run, packages' `initialize` among them, so reaching it is
the probe: it prints `probe: ok`, then `launched-by <launcher>` (or
`launched-by none`), then `bundle <path> identifier <id>` from
CoreFoundation's main bundle (`Ffi.mainBundle`, `identifier none` when it
has none), or `bundle none` where there is none, then `executable <its own
path>`, so a check sees which executable a launcher chose
(`Restart.probeLines`); it removes the hand-over record it was handed, so
a cold start under the launcher's watch that probes leaves none
(`Restart.Launch.probeRemoves`), and returns 0. The launch
flags (`Editor/Restart/Launch.lean`) are stripped before the mode is chosen
(`Editor/Executable.lean`); `run` adds the running executable
(`IO.appPath`) and hands the launch to the headless and window modes.

`editor headless [--render] [--generate] [<sysroot> <olean dir>] <config dir>
<script file>` is the headless mode (`Editor/Headless.lean`): it loads the config in a
directory, runs a script against it and prints what happened, for
integration tests; with `--render` what it publishes is drawn by the
library's headless render session. The toolchain's directory and the
editor's `.olean` directory may be left out in an app bundle, which holds
both (`Editor/ModuleLookup.lean`). `run` hands it the rest of the command
line after `headless`, and the headless mode reads that itself.

`editor window-check [--seconds <n>] [--no-ime]` is the keyboard check
(`Editor/WindowCheck.lean`): it opens a window and reports what of the
keyboard reached a Lean loop. A person runs it; nothing in the gate does.

Any other command line is the window mode's,
`editor [--config <dir>] [--sysroot <dir>] [--oleans <dir>] [--no-ime] [<file>]`
(`Editor/Window.lean`, `Editor/Window/Real.lean`): the editor in a window,
on a file, or on its one empty buffer when none is given. An empty command
line is that, which is how Finder starts the app. So a file called `demo`,
`headless` or `window-check` is opened as `./demo`. A command line the
window mode does not take, an option it does not know or two files, is
reported with its usage, with the exit status 2.

## This module names no package, and not the config base

It is a module of the host: both hosts register features only from the
packages they are handed (`Editor/Package.lean`), and `run` takes them as
a value and passes them to the headless mode and to the window mode. The
executable's root is the one place the packages are named. That root is
generated by the package-set build from the entry modules of the set's
Lake packages (`lakefile.lean`, the facet `editor`;
`docs/design/packages.md`, "The package set and its build"), and it is one
call of `run`. No module of a host imports an entry module or a module
that defines a package, which `tools/host-imports-check.sh` checks on
Lake's module graph.

It does not import the config base, `Editor` (`Editor.lean`), either: the
base is not a core module, and this is one (`Editor/Core.lean` imports it).
The elaborator adapter the hosts bring
(`Editor/Config/Elaborate.lean`, "The base") imports the base's modules
from their `.olean` files at runtime, and each must already be linked into
the executable, so that the import runs no initialiser. So the root that
calls `run` imports the base beside this module, for the link alone, and
`tools/core-imports-check.sh` checks that every root that links the
adapter does.

## The base

Config is elaborated on a base of modules, and the base is the package
set's, handed to `run` as names: `Editor`, the core's base, then the entry
module of each package of the set outside the core, in the order they
register (`<P>.EditorPackage`; `docs/design/packages.md`, "The package set
and its build"). So config can name what a package of the set declares:
the entry module imports the package's modules. The generated root writes
the list and imports each module of it, so each is linked. The core's own
entry module is not in it: what config sees of the core is what `Editor`
imports.

`run` passes the base to the headless mode and to the window mode, which
hand it to the elaborator with the directories its modules are found in
(`Editor/ModuleLookup.lean`): outside a bundle those the build recorded for
the executable, one a package, and in a bundle the bundle's one. A set with
no package beside the core's hands `Editor` alone.

## The input scheme

The hosts run the input scheme they are handed (`Headless.main`,
`Window.main`), and `run` hands them the one its package set supplies: the
generated root passes `run` the `scheme` of each entry module whose Lake
package declares the keyword `editor-input-scheme`, in the order the
packages register, and the last is the one run (`SchemePackage.chosen?`,
`Editor/InputScheme.lean`, "The scheme a package supplies").

A set that supplies none has an editor that refuses to start: the headless
mode and the window mode print `editor: no input scheme is installed: no
package of this editor's package set supplies one` on stderr and exit 1.
So does `--probe`, so that a restart into such a set is given up before the
session is handed over, with that line as its reason
(`Editor/Restore/Restart.lean`), and does not leave the user with an editor
that will not start. `demo`, `--version` and `window-check` run no scheme
and are not refused. Which modes are refused is pure
(`Executable.Mode.needsScheme`, pinned by `ModelTest.executableModeChecks`);
the choice and the text by `ModelTest.schemePackageChecks`; and the gate
runs the empty set's editor, which has no scheme once evil is a package
(`mise run ffi-test`).

## The generated files

A config directory is a Lake package, so that Lean's language server checks
a config file as the editor elaborates it
(`Editor/Config/LakePackage.lean`). The editor creates the directory's
`lakefile.toml` and `lean-toolchain` when they are absent, and what it
writes them from comes with it: the generated root hands `run` the Lake
name of the package set and the text of the core's `lean-toolchain`
(`Config.LakePackage.Origin`), and `run` passes them to the two hosts. The
set's directory is not handed in: an editor finds it at each start
(`Editor/ModuleLookup.lean`, "The set"), and one that finds none writes no
lakefile. The window mode generates the files; the headless mode only when
its command line says `--generate`.
-/

namespace Editor.Main

/-- The command the executable runs: it makes a text, appends to it and reads
it back. `Editor.append` is the command the model tests run against the pure
model (`ModelTest.commandProperties`); here the same source runs against the
Rust library. -/
def greeting : Editor.TextM String := do
  let text ← Editor.createText "héllo"
  Editor.append text " wörld"
  text.read

/-- Calls the Rust library, prints the results, and returns non-zero if one is
not what it should be. -/
def demo : IO UInt32 := do
  let sum := Editor.Ffi.add 2 3
  IO.println s!"Rust says 2 + 3 = {sum}"
  let leanOnMain ← Editor.Ffi.isMainThread
  IO.println s!"Lean's main is on the process's main thread: {leanOnMain}"
  let serviceOnMain ← Editor.Ffi.mainThreadProbe
  IO.println s!"The main-thread service is on the process's main thread: {serviceOnMain}"
  let appended ←
    match (← greeting.runReal (← Editor.RealTexts.new)).1 with
    | .ok text =>
      IO.println s!"A command appended to a text held by Rust: {text}"
      pure (text == "héllo wörld")
    | .error refusal =>
      IO.println s!"A command run against the Rust library was refused: {repr refusal}"
      pure false
  return if sum == 5 && !leanOnMain && serviceOnMain && appended then 0 else 1

/-- The mark of `run`: it runs a real editor loop thread (`Editor/RealHost.lean`). -/
def run.realHost : RealHost :=
  .loop

/-- Runs the mode the command line `args` selects (`Executable.mode`),
with `packages` as the packages the hosts register, `schemes` as the input
schemes the package set supplies and `base` as the modules config is
elaborated on ("The base", in the module docs). The match is exhaustive on
purpose: it must act on every mode.

A mode that runs an input scheme (`Executable.Mode.needsScheme`: the
headless mode, the window mode and the probe) is refused when `schemes` is
empty: `editor: ` and `SchemePackage.noneInstalled` on stderr, and status
1, before the mode reads its command line ("The input scheme", in the
module docs). Otherwise the hosts run the scheme chosen of `schemes`
(`SchemePackage.chosen?`).

With `demo`, the demonstration. With `--version`, the build's version, build
ID and commit on stdout and status 0, or, when its bundle's `Info.plist`
cannot be read, why on stderr and status 1. With `headless`, the headless mode, handed
the rest of the command line, which exits with its own status: 0 when the
script ran to its end, 1 when it could not, and 2 for a command line it does
not take. With `window-check`, the keyboard check, which opens a window and
exits with its own status: 0 when the window ran and 1 when it could not be
opened. Any other command line, an empty one too, is the window mode's,
which opens a window, on a file when one is given, and exits 0 when the
window ran, 1 when it could not be opened, and 2, with its usage, for a
command line it does not take. The headless mode and the window mode are
each handed `packages`, `base` and `origin`, the set's Lake name and the
toolchain pin, from which they generate a config directory's lakefile and
toolchain file ("The generated files", in the module docs). -/
def run (packages : Array Package) (schemes : Array SchemePackage) (base : Array Lean.Name)
    (origin : Config.LakePackage.Origin) (args : List String) : IO UInt32 := do
  let mode := Editor.Executable.mode args
  let refused : IO UInt32 := do
    IO.eprintln s!"editor: {SchemePackage.noneInstalled}"
    return 1
  let chosen := SchemePackage.chosen? schemes
  -- Before the mode reads the rest of its command line, so that a usage
  -- error does not hide it.
  if mode.needsScheme && chosen.isNone then
    return ← refused
  match mode with
  | .demo =>
    demo
  | .version =>
    let readFile (path : System.FilePath) : IO (Except String String) := do
      try
        return .ok (← IO.FS.readFile path)
      catch error =>
        return .error (toString error)
    let (text, status) :=
      Editor.Version.report (← Editor.Version.read (← IO.appPath).toString readFile)
    if status == 0 then
      IO.println text
    else
      IO.eprintln text
    pure status
  | .probe launch =>
    for line in Restart.probeLines launch (← IO.appPath).toString (← Ffi.mainBundle) do
      IO.println line
    if let some record := launch.probeRemoves then
      try
        IO.FS.removeFile record
      catch _ =>
        pure ()
    pure 0
  | .headless launch rest =>
    let some supplied := chosen | refused
    let launch := { launch with running := some (← IO.appPath) }
    Editor.Headless.main supplied.scheme packages base origin launch rest
  | .windowCheck options =>
    Editor.WindowCheck.main options
  | .window launch arguments =>
    let some supplied := chosen | refused
    let launch := { launch with running := some (← IO.appPath) }
    Editor.Window.main supplied.scheme packages base origin launch arguments

end Editor.Main
